<td id="S3.T1.8.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding:0.9pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.9.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">2.10M</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.9.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding:0.9pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.9.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">2.36M</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.9.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding:0.9pt 3.0pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.9.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">384M</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Experiments</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train two baseline models using all the ASR, T2TT and S2TT data described in § <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Data ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, with direct (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<sub id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Base</span></sub></span>) and CoT (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Base</span></sub></span>) prompting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the impact of scaling S2TT data, we incrementally add S2TT<sub id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> samples to each baseline, training 5 new systems with 20%, 40%, 60%, 80% and 100% of the full dataset respectively. We refer to these models as <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<sub id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">augXX</span></sub></span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">augXX</span></sub></span>, where <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">xx</span> is the percentage of pseudo-labeled data used. These models are trained independently, they are not checkpoints of a single experiment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the CoT setting, each speech–transcription pair in the pseudo-labeled data is repeated six times, one for each target language. We hypothesize this repetition may bias the model and harm the ASR performance, which is particularly critical for CoT prompting. To validate this, we train two variants of <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">augXX</span></sub></span>: in <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sup id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">†</span></sup><sub id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text">augXX</span></sub></span>, the transcription step contributes to the loss, whereas in <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">augXX</span></sub></span> it does not.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the models using Word Error Rate (WER) for ASR, and with BLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, computed with SacreBLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>,<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            signature: <span id="footnote4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nrefs:1|case:mixed|eff:no|tok:13a|smooth: exp|version:2.5.1</span></span></span></span> as well as x<span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">comet</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite><span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/Unbabel/XCOMET-XL" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">https://huggingface.co/Unbabel/XCOMET-XL</a></span></span></span> for T2TT and S2TT.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03093v1/s2tt.svg" id="S3.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/307;" width="476" height="307"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">S2TT</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03093v1/asr.svg" id="S3.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/307;" width="476" height="307"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">ASR</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03093v1/t2tt.svg" id="S3.F3.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/300;" width="476" height="300"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">T2TT</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance on Fleurs (x<span id="S3.F3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">comet</span>) when scaling the amount of S2TT<sub id="S3.F3.6.2" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> data. Results are averaged across all the considered language directions.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Training Details</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both stage 1 and stage 2 last one epoch and employ AdamW optimizer, a cosine learning rate scheduler, and gradient clipping with a maximum norm of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In stage 1, we use a learning rate of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\cdot 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\cdot 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, a warm up during the first <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\%</annotation></semantics></math> of updates, and set a maximum sequence length of 1024. Tokens from different sequences are packed into full-length sequences to improve efficiency, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Training is run on 16 GPUs with a per-device batch size of 64, yielding an effective batch size of 256.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In stage 2, we increase the maximum sequence length to 2048 (without sequence packing) and lower the learning rate to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\cdot 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\cdot 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, with warmup over the first <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> of updates. We train on 16 GPUs with a per-device batch size of 32, resulting in an effective batch size of 512.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are implemented in the <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">transformers</span> library with DeepSpeed for distributed training, and run on NVIDIA H100 GPUs. To optimize memory usage, we apply mixed precision (bfloat16), gradient checkpointing, and Liger Kernel, which further improves efficiency and throughput.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initial <span id="S4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Base</span></sub></span> superiority</span>  We first verify that our baselines reproduce trends reported in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. As expected, Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Results ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that the <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Base</span></sub></span> outperforms the <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<sub id="S4.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Base</span></sub></span> across all languages, confirming the benefit of decomposing the task into transcription and translation in this scenario. The average baseline gap is approximately 5 BLEU points and 7 x<span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">comet</span> points.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Baselines evaluation on FLEURS.</figcaption>
