</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<span id="S4.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> scales better than </span>CoT</span>  Figure <a href="#S3.F3.sf1" title="In Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Experiments ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a> shows that the two <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> variants peak at 20% of the S2TT<sub id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> data and then degrade as more data is added. In contrast, <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<sub id="S4.p2.1.4.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> consistently improves, achieving a better score with <span id="S4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<sub id="S4.p2.1.5.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p2.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug100</span></sub></span>. Although the peaks of <span id="S4.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sup id="S4.p2.1.6.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.p2.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">†</span></sup><sub id="S4.p2.1.6.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p2.1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> and <span id="S4.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p2.1.7.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p2.1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> are still higher, <span id="S4.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<sub id="S4.p2.1.8.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p2.1.8.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> demonstrates a steadier scaling trend, suggesting that this gap could be closed with additional data. These results indicate a potential advantage of direct prompting when sufficient S2TT<sub id="S4.p2.1.9" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> data is available.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<span id="S4.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> degrades ASR as data scales</span></span>  Figure <a href="#S3.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Experiments ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a> shows that WER remains stable with <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<sub id="S4.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> across all S2TT<sub id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> data scales, whereas both <span id="S4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p3.1.4.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p3.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> and <span id="S4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sup id="S4.p3.1.5.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.p3.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">†</span></sup><sub id="S4.p3.1.5.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p3.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> gradually increase it by up to <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+7.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>7.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+7.4</annotation></semantics></math>% and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+13</annotation></semantics></math>% relative to their baseline. These trends correlate with the steady decrease of S2TT performance, reflecting their critical reliance on the ASR sub-task. This is also observed at <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>% of S2TT<sub id="S4.p3.1.6" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> data, where <span id="S4.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p3.1.7.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p3.1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> reaches its minimum WER alongside its highest x<span id="S4.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">comet</span> score. Additionally, <span id="S4.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p3.1.9.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p3.1.9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Base</span></sub></span> shows a WER <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>% lower than its counterpart strategy. This confirms the findings of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, indicating that direct S2TT data improve ASR performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F3.sf3" title="In Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Experiments ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(c)</span></a> shows that <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct</span> performs slightly worse on T2TT, likely due to the lower proportion of this task in the training recipe (in <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<sub id="S4.p4.1.2.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span>, S2TT<sub id="S4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> samples do not contain the T2TT sub-task). Nevertheless, performance for all three methods remains stable around +2 x<span id="S4.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">comet</span> points. These results make <span id="S4.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct</span> appealing, showing that ASR and T2TT remain stable as pseudo-labeled S2TT data scales.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03093v1/s2tt_en.svg" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/300;" width="476" height="300"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">en<math id="S4.F4.sf1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>x<span id="S4.F4.sf1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> and </span>x<math id="S4.F4.sf1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>en</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03093v1/s2tt_ca.svg" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/307;" width="476" height="307"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">ca<math id="S4.F4.sf2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>x<span id="S4.F4.sf2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> and </span>x<math id="S4.F4.sf2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>ca</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03093v1/s2tt_it.svg" id="S4.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/300;" width="476" height="300"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">it<math id="S4.F4.sf3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>x<span id="S4.F4.sf3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> and </span>x<math id="S4.F4.sf3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>it</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">S2TT results evaluated on Fleurs test set with x<span id="S4.F4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">comet</span>. The x-axis represents the percentage of the S2TT<sub id="S4.F4.8.2" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> data with which the different <span id="S4.F4.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct</span> and <span id="S4.F4.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT</span> models were trained.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sup id="S4.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.p5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">†</span></sup><sub id="S4.p5.1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub><span id="S4.p5.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> lags behind </span>CoT<sub id="S4.p5.1.1.4" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p5.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span>  Figures <a href="#S3.F3.sf1" title="In Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Experiments ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F3.sf2" title="In Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Experiments ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a> support the hypothesis from § <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Experiments ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> that training the transcription step may negatively impact the model, as <span id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sup id="S4.p5.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.p5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">†</span></sup><sub id="S4.p5.1.2.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p5.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> consistently underperforms <span id="S4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p5.1.3.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> in ASR and S2TT. Nonetheless, <span id="S4.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p5.1.4.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p5.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug</span></sub></span> also leads to some degradation. A possible explanation is that, by not training the transcription step, all S2TT<sub id="S4.p5.1.5" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> data is treated as T2TT with speech as context, effectively reducing the proportion of the ASR task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results are consistent across languages</span>  To assess whether these results hold across languages, Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4 Results ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows S2TT performance for <span id="S4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en</span>, <span id="S4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ca</span>, and <span id="S4.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span>. We selected these languages because <span id="S4.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en</span> benefits from the the majority of ASR data and typically achieves strong translation performance due to the abundance of English-centric datasets. The other two are extremes in S2TT<sub id="S4.p6.1.6" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> data quantity (see Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Data ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>): <span id="S4.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ca</span> has the most and <span id="S4.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> the least.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4 Results ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> shows that <span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en</span> follows a similar trend to Figure <a href="#S3.F3.sf1" title="In Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Experiments ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Revisiting Direct Speech-to-Text Translation with Speech LLMs: Better Scaling than CoT Prompting?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>, though performance on <span id="S4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en<math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>x</span> remains nearly constant. This is likely due to the large amount of ASR data in <span id="S4.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en</span>, which, similarly to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, allows <span id="S4.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<sub id="S4.p7.1.4.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p7.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Base</span></sub></span> (at 0% S2TT<sub id="S4.p7.1.5" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> data) to perform close to <span id="S4.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p7.1.6.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p7.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Base</span></sub></span>. Both <span id="S4.p7.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ca</span> and <span id="S4.p7.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">it</span> exhibit the same overall trend, confirming that the results are consistent across languages. Notably, for <span id="S4.p7.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ca<math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>x</span>, <span id="S4.p7.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct<sub id="S4.p7.1.10.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p7.1.10.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug100</span></sub></span> nearly matches the peak of <span id="S4.p7.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT<sub id="S4.p7.1.11.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S4.p7.1.11.1.1" class="ltx_text">aug20</span></sub></span>. This supports the hypothesis that, with sufficient S2TT<sub id="S4.p7.1.12" class="ltx_sub">pl</sub> data, <span id="S4.p7.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct</span> can reach <span id="S4.p7.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT</span> performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusions and Future Directions</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we systematically compare <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT</span> and <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct</span> prompting strategies for S2TT using pseudo-labeled data, which has become the standard approach for scaling this task. Our experiments show that <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT</span> struggles to improve with more data, regardless of whether the transcription step is trained or not. In contrast, <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct</span> prompting demonstrates a consistent scaling trend: although its absolute performance has not yet surpassed <span id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT</span>, it steadily improves as more pseudo-labeled data is added, indicating strong potential for larger scale trainings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These findings position <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct</span> prompting as a promising strategy for future research. Beyond its favorable scaling behavior, <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct</span> also offers practical advantages: it requires roughly half the computational resources of <span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT</span> and is simpler to implement. Moreover, the observed trends raise important questions about its limits: it remains to be validated whether <span id="S5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct</span> could not only match but eventually surpass <span id="S5.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT</span> with an even larger scale of data, clarifying its potential as a primary strategy for S2TT training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, beyond the current setup, a particularly relevant direction is paralinguistic-aware translation. E2E S2TT systems are not constrained by the transcription bottleneck, which gives them the potential to produce richer translations that reflect linguistic cues uniquely present in speech, such as prosody. However, because current training largely relies on ASR and T2TT datasets, especially in CoT prompting, this capacity is unlikely to emerge naturally. Progress may therefore require training with direct translations annotated for speech-specific cues, and it would be valuable to investigate whether <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Direct</span> prompting can better exploit the full richness of the speech signal compared to <span id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoT</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work is funded by the Ministerio para la Transformación Digital y de la Función Pública and Plan de Recuperación, Transformación y Resiliencia – Funded by EU – NextGenerationEU within the framework of the project Modelos del Lenguaje. FC and CEB acknowledge their AI4S fellowship within the “Generación D” initiative by Red.es, Ministerio para la Transformación Digital y de la Función Pública, for talent attraction (C005/24-ED CV1), funded by NextGenerationEU through PRTR.</p>
</div>
</section>
